{
  "index": 72,
  "text": "In cases of heterotropia, the degree of misalignment may exceed the patient\u2019s fusion vergence capacity (32). Our study included patients across various stages of glaucoma, correlating with different severities of visual field loss. Research shows that loss of peripheral fusion from glaucomatous damage can degrade fusional ability, leading to diplopia (33). This occurs more frequently in patients with mild visual field loss, whereas those with advanced glaucoma and severe visual field defects may not notice diplopia, potentially leading to underreporting of symptoms (34). Thus, the absence of reported double vision does not necessarily imply unaffected ocular motility.",
